body:
A driver is a throwaway [[main()]] written for one purpose: to call a single
[[Function]] with known inputs and print what comes back. It is how you test a function
before the rest of the program exists.

```c++
// driver for is_prime()
int main() {
	int tests[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 17, 25, 97};
	for (int i = 0; i < 7; i++)
		cout << tests[i] << " -> " << is_prime(tests[i]) << endl;
	return 0;
}
```

This is the **bottom-up** half of [[debugging]]: test the lowest-level functions on their
own, then the functions that call them, then `main()`. The top-down half goes the other
way and needs a *stub* instead, a fake function that returns a hardcoded value so the
caller can be tested before the callee is written. Driver and stub are the two sides of
the same trick.

Picking the test values is the whole skill: normal cases, boundary cases (0, 1, the
first and last element), and the cases that should fail. Same instinct as
[[Unit testing]] in [[INTRODUCTION TO SOFTWARE ENGINEERING]], which is this idea with a
framework around it so the tests survive past the afternoon.

